---

<BaseLayout
  title="Matthew Cochran — Product Strategy & Hands-on Software Engineering"
  description="Matthew Cochran connects product strategy, requirements, and hands-on software engineering. Explore his work, open-source tools, and practical field notes."
>
  <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <div class="shell hero-layout">
      <div class="hero-copy reveal">
        <p class="kicker">Product &amp; engineering dossier <span>New York / Remote</span></p>
        <h1 id="hero-title">Product strategy and engineering, <em>working together.</em></h1>
        <p class="hero-lede">I connect product strategy with engineering so teams can make deliberate choices about what to build, when to build it, and how to keep it dependable.</p>
        <p class="hero-context">Unclear direction and requirements can turn engineering effort into rework. I focus on keeping the customer's problem, the business decision, and the engineering work connected—from the first assumption through requirements, handoffs, and verification.</p>
        <p class="hero-context">I've led both product and engineering teams at artnet and OutboundLabs, and held Staff Engineer roles at Distributed Spectrum and Qrypt. I stay hands-on with architecture and software development, balancing product needs with reliability, security, maintainability, and operating cost.</p>
        <p class="hero-context">Today, I apply that approach through MC Product Labs and open-source tools that help clarify work, coordinate execution, and check the result.</p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button primary" href="#selected-work">Explore the work</a>
          <a class="hero-text-cta" href="#junior">Open source <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
          <a class="hero-text-cta" href="/resume/">View résumé <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="hero-profile reveal delay-1" aria-label="Profile summary">
        <div class="portrait-frame"><img src="/assets/images/MC-headshot.png" alt="Matthew Cochran" /></div>
        <dl class="profile-facts"><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>Valley Stream, NY / Remote</dd></div><div><dt>Current work</dt><dd>Junior Agent · Jevitate · opv · MC Product Labs</dd></div></dl>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="selected-work" class="section shell" aria-labelledby="selected-work-title">
    <header class="section-heading reveal"><div><p class="kicker">Examples and evidence</p><h2 id="selected-work-title">Work you can inspect.</h2></div><p>Follow a concrete example, inspect the tools, or read an attributed account of the work.</p></header>
    <div class="outcomes">
      <article class="outcome reveal"><p class="eyebrow">Browser investigation</p><h3>When Saved does not mean saved</h3><div><p>A deliberately broken example shows what to inspect when a success message hides a failed request.</p><a href="/blog/jevitate-launch/">Follow the Jevitate example →</a></div></article>
      <article class="outcome reveal"><p class="eyebrow">Delegated coding</p><h3>Keep the handoff reviewable</h3><div><p>Inspect how Junior Agent scopes work and keeps independent checks and results available for review.</p><a href="https://junioragent.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Explore Junior Agent ↗</a></div></article>
      <article class="outcome reveal"><p class="eyebrow">Platform modernization</p><h3>Read the external account</h3><div><p>Persistent's case study describes artnet's migration to Google Cloud with MediaAgility.</p><a href="https://www.persistent.com/client-success/artnet-increases-efficiency-by-280x-as-they-move-to-google-cloud/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the case study ↗</a></div></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="operating-band" aria-label="Operating disciplines">
    <div class="shell operating-grid">
      <article><span>01 / Product Discovery</span><h2>Governed Strategy</h2><p>Test the assumption that could change a product decision before committing more engineering time. Carry the result into clear requirements and an explicit next step.</p></article>
      <article><span>02 / Project Execution</span><h2>Product and engineering execution</h2><p>Keep the reason for the work visible through requirements, dependencies, and handoffs. Make room for engineering feedback and the technical needs that support the business over time.</p></article>
      <article><span>03 / Technical Implementation</span><h2>Safety Boundaries</h2><p>Building explicit tool permissions, human approval controls, and independent verification into AI-assisted development workflows.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="product-company" class="section shell" aria-labelledby="product-company-title">
    <header class="section-heading reveal">
      <div><p class="kicker">Product company</p><h2 id="product-company-title">Products built and stewarded through <em>MC Product Labs</em>.</h2></div>
      <p>Through MC Product Labs, I start with a narrow customer group and a painful problem, then test the assumptions that could change the next investment. The aim is to avoid building before the need is understood and to carry what we learn into clear engineering work. The company blog covers the product work; my personal writing covers engineering and open-source projects.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="product-company-actions reveal">
      <a class="button primary" href="https://mcproductlabs.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Explore MC Product Labs <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </div>
    <div class="product-grid">
      {featuredCompanyProducts.map((product, index) => (
        <article class="product-card reveal" style={`--order:${index}`}>
          <div class="card-meta"><span>{product.type}</span><strong>{product.status}</strong></div>
          <h3>{product.name}</h3>
          <p>{product.description}</p>
          <ul class="evidence-list">{product.evidence.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul>
          <a href={product.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{product.cta} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

<section id="junior" class="family family-dark"><div class="shell family-layout"><header class="family-intro reveal"><p class="kicker">Open Source · Junior Agent</p><h2>Scoped coding tasks.<br /><em>Reviewable results.</em></h2><p>Junior Agent delegates bounded coding work to lower-cost models using acceptance criteria, isolated Git worktrees, independent checks, and bounded repair attempts. I actively use it across my own projects.</p><div class="family-cta"><a class="button primary" href="https://junioragent.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Visit Junior Agent ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/matt-cochran/junior" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View source ↗</a></div></header><div class="family-modules"><article><span>01</span><h3>Explicit task contracts</h3><p>Scope and acceptance criteria define what each delegated task must deliver.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Independent checks</h3><p>Verification and bounded repair attempts produce evidence for review.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Reviewable handoffs</h3><p>Changes, check results, model usage, and unresolved work remain visible to the reviewer.</p></article><article><span>04</span><h3>Praxec tooling</h3><p>Earlier MCP/CLI tools for critical-path planning, House of Quality analysis, and failure-mode assessment support task planning and review.</p></article></div></div></section>
  <section id="praxec" class="family family-dark">
    <div class="shell family-layout">
      <header class="family-intro reveal">
        <p class="kicker">Earlier open source · Praxec</p>
        <h2>Safe AI requires <em>deterministic</em> boundaries.</h2>
        <p>Praxec is an open-source agentic harness—hosting workflows, enforcing policy gates, and retaining control over state, tools, approvals, and evidence while models do probabilistic work.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/praxec" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Explore the Praxec organization ↗</a>
      </header>
      <div class="family-modules">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>The harness</h3><p>Hosts agentic workflows with legal transitions, durable policy gates, human approval, and audit trails—authority in code, not model interpretation.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>Cognitive architectures</h3><p>Structured, governed approaches for discovery, analysis, FMECA, implementation, and verification.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>Deterministic MCP tools</h3><p>Open-source utilities—CPM planning, elicitation, scientific process, cross-matrix analysis, corpus work, and operational tools—that plug into the harness.</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><h3>FrontRails integration</h3><p>Praxec’s MCP and CLI utilities also support Junior Agent task planning and review, including critical-path planning, House of Quality analysis, and failure-mode assessment.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="tflo" class="family family-light dossier-grid">
    <div class="shell family-layout">
      <header class="family-intro reveal">
        <p class="kicker">Family Study: Temporal Runtimes</p>
        <h2>Event-processing for <em>distributed</em> systems.</h2>
        <p>The tflo family explores embeddable temporal event processing across Rust, WASM, React, and browser environments—with shared models and deterministic behavior as design goals.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/matt-cochran?tab=repositories&q=tflo" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Explore tflo repositories ↗</a>
      </header>
      <div class="tflo-stack">
        <article><div><span>Runtime</span><h3>tflo-core</h3></div><p>The embeddable Rust engine and shared temporal foundation.</p></article>
        <article><div><span>Interface</span><h3>tflo-react</h3></div><p>Declarative temporal state for React applications backed by the shared runtime.</p></article>
        <article><div><span>Interaction</span><h3>tflo-browser-events</h3></div><p>Browser-event capture and signals designed around durable identifiers and pluggable sinks.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="jevitate" class="family family-dark">
    <div class="shell family-layout">
      <header class="family-intro reveal">
        <p class="kicker">Open Source · Jevitate</p>
        <h2>Autonomous browser testing with a <em>deterministic</em> spine.</h2>
        <p>Jevitate records real browser sessions into typed Journeys over Playwright, then replays, explores, load-tests, and reviews them from a single CLI or MCP server. Autonomous runs are driven by TypeSafe&rsquo;s Jev and bounded by hard guardrails&mdash;model decisions are hypotheses; runtime evidence decides pass or fail. MIT-licensed, TypeScript, Node 20+.</p>
        <div class="family-cta">
          <a class="button primary" href="https://jevitate.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Visit Jevitate <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/matt-cochran/jevitate" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View source ↗</a>
          <a class="text-link" href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@jevitate/cli" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">npm i -g @jevitate/cli ↗</a>
        </div>
      </header>
      <div class="family-modules">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>Record &rarr; replay</h3><p>Capture a real flow by demonstration into a deterministic, typed Journey&mdash;then parameterize, promote, and load-test it against an authorized origin.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>Goal-directed exploration</h3><p>Drive to a natural-language goal with autonomous exploration; success is judged by an independent assertion, never the model&rsquo;s say-so.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>Adversarial &amp; UX review</h3><p>Bounded misuse against a trusted hard-signal defect oracle, plus ranked, cited, evidence-anchored usability findings.</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><h3>MCP server, safety-first</h3><p>Exposes only an allowlisted tool surface for agents; every run is origin-bound and budget-capped, and credentials are never sent to a model.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

<section id="opv" class="family family-light"><div class="shell family-layout"><header class="family-intro reveal"><p class="kicker">Open Source · opv</p><h2>Secrets delivery with <em>explicit controls</em>.</h2><p>opv (1password-vault) is an open-source Rust CLI connecting 1Password-managed secrets to application runtimes and CI. 1Password remains the source of secret values; opv coordinates validation and delivery through official CLIs.</p><div class="family-cta"><a class="text-link" href="https://opv.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Visit opv ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/matt-cochran/1password-vault" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View opv on GitHub ↗</a></div></header><div class="family-modules"><article><span>01</span><h3>Typed validation</h3><p>Declare key names, IDs, and validation rules. Reporting identifies problems without displaying secret values.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Plan before sync</h3><p>Review proposed changes and bind synchronization to a plan. Deployment and pruning require explicit controls.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Runtime and CI targets</h3><p>Supports Fly.io, Azure Key Vault and Container Apps, Kubernetes, External Secrets Operator, and GitHub Actions.</p></article><article><span>04</span><h3>Automation and assistants</h3><p>Structured JSON reports, stable error codes, and an agent skill support scripted and AI-assisted workflows. Distributed as prebuilt binaries and through npm; MIT licensed.</p></article></div></div></section>
  <section id="proof" class="section shell" aria-labelledby="proof-title">
    <header class="section-heading reveal">
      <div><p class="kicker">Engineering / Attributed Evidence</p><h2 id="proof-title">Engineering &amp;<br />Attributed Evidence</h2></div>
      <p>Breadth is useful when it becomes pattern recognition: across public companies, billion-dollar enterprise software, regulated domains, and early-stage teams.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="outcomes">
      {outcomes.map((outcome, index) => (
        <article class="outcome reveal" style={`--order:${index}`}>
          <p class="eyebrow">{outcome.eyebrow}</p><h3>{outcome.title}</h3><p>{outcome.copy}</p>
          {outcome.link && <a href={outcome.link} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read external case study ↗</a>}
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div class="proof-actions"><a class="button secondary" href="/resume/">View full résumé →</a><a class="text-link" href="/about/">More about Matthew →</a></div>
  </section>

  <section id="method" class="methodology">
    <div class="method-grid" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="shell method-content reveal">
      <p class="kicker">Working method / 03</p>
      <h2>Conceive the product.<br />Steward the system.</h2>
      <p>Discovery, boundary analysis, critical-path planning, implementation, and verification form one accountable delivery loop. The artifacts change; the discipline does not.</p>
      <div class="method-steps">
        <span>Discover</span><i>→</i><span>Model risk</span><i>→</i><span>Plan the path</span><i>→</i><span>Ship</span><i>→</i><span>Verify</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="engage" class="engagement shell reveal">
    <div><span class="status-stamp">Decisions and field notes</span><h2>Explore the work behind the approach.</h2></div>
    <div><p>Read practical examples of product decisions, engineering tradeoffs, and software verification. My professional background and project history are available in the résumé.</p><div class="engagement-links"><a class="button primary" href="/blog/">Read the field notes →</a><a class="text-link" href="/resume/">View résumé</a><a class="text-link" href="mailto:matthew@cochranweb.com">Start a conversation</a></div></div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
